1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,925
Loopback Transformer:

2
00:00:01,925 --> 00:00:05,150
towards a unified view of
residual looped Transformers.

3
00:00:05,950 --> 00:00:09,200
Looped Transformers apply the
same blocks again and again.

4
00:00:09,238 --> 00:00:10,850
Sharing fixes the weights,

5
00:00:10,850 --> 00:00:13,225
but not how each update is reused.

6
00:00:14,450 --> 00:00:16,650
Addition gives every update weight one,

7
00:00:16,650 --> 00:00:18,763
so the total grows with depth.

8
00:00:18,763 --> 00:00:21,325
A joint softmax shares one unit of weight,

9
00:00:21,325 --> 00:00:23,075
so the input shrinks.

10
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:25,000
Loopback keeps the input at one;

11
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:26,425
decay sets the total,

12
00:00:26,425 --> 00:00:28,200
and attention allocates it.

13
00:00:30,450 --> 00:00:31,312
The recipe:

14
00:00:31,312 --> 00:00:32,137
store updates,

15
00:00:32,137 --> 00:00:33,275
not outputs;

16
00:00:33,275 --> 00:00:35,387
keep the input on its own path;

17
00:00:35,387 --> 00:00:36,725
let decay set the total,

18
00:00:36,725 --> 00:00:38,375
and attention allocate it.

19
00:00:39,450 --> 00:00:43,288
From 191M to 3B parameters,

20
00:00:43,288 --> 00:00:47,575
Loopback reaches lower validation
loss than the standard Transformer,

21
00:00:47,575 --> 00:00:50,775
with 40–75% of its parameters.

22
00:00:51,450 --> 00:00:53,712
At 374M,

23
00:00:53,712 --> 00:00:57,975
it is the best of nine models in
both loss and thirteen-task mean.

24
00:00:59,950 --> 00:01:02,575
The read costs a fraction
of a percent in FLOPs,

25
00:01:02,575 --> 00:01:04,050
the same KV cache,

26
00:01:04,050 --> 00:01:05,375
and smaller weights.

27
00:01:06,950 --> 00:01:08,038
Store updates.

28
00:01:08,038 --> 00:01:10,000
Keep the input on its own path.

29
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:11,400
Decay sets the total;

30
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:13,000
attention allocates it.

